[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: web_server_2, web_server_3
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: The HTTPS (TLS) service layer on the web servers is not running/listening. Specifically, Apache on web_server_2 and web_server_3 has no SSL/443 listener configured.
- Mechanism: The user’s browser likely attempts HTTPS by default (HTTPS-First behavior or bookmark/protocol auto-upgrade). TCP connections to 10.200.0.5:443 (web2.local) and 10.200.0.6:443 (web3.local) are refused by the host stack, so the page “spins” and then fails to load, while plain HTTP works.
- Localization: Application layer on web_server_2 and web_server_3. Evidence:
  • From host_1_1_1_1: curl http://web2.local and http://web3.local return HTTP/1.1 200 OK immediately.
  • From host_1_1_1_1: curl -vk https://web2.local and https://web3.local fail with “connect … port 443 failed: Connection refused.”
  • On web_server_2 and web_server_3: netstat shows only 0.0.0.0:80 LISTEN, no process bound to 443.
  • DNS resolution is correct (web2.local→10.200.0.5, web3.local→10.200.0.6), and ICMP/L3 reachability is healthy.
- Root cause: HTTPS is not enabled/configured on web_server_2 and web_server_3 (no Apache SSL virtual hosts/mod_ssl or certificates), so port 443 is closed.
- Corrective fix: Configure and enable HTTPS on both servers (enable mod_ssl, create SSL vhosts for web2.local and web3.local on port 443 with valid certs/keys, then restart Apache). No network changes are required. As a short-term workaround, instruct users to access http://web2.local and http://web3.local explicitly until HTTPS is configured.

=== END ===